Subject: [PATCH] Pcsx2Defs: allow 16KB host pages on Linux aarch64

The aarch64 host page size is fixed at compile time: 16KB for Apple
Silicon, 4KB for everything else. A Linux kernel built with 16KB pages
(Raspberry Pi 5) then runs a core whose vtlb page protection is built
for 4KB pages, and the mprotect calls behind it fail or cover the
wrong range without any error being reported.

Let a build select the 16KB layout that Apple Silicon already uses by
defining PCSX2_HOST_PAGE_16K. Without the define nothing changes.

--- a/common/Pcsx2Defs.h
+++ b/common/Pcsx2Defs.h
@@ -55,8 +55,10 @@
 	#error Unsupported architecture
 #endif
 
-#if (defined(_M_ARM64) || defined(__aarch64__)) && defined(__APPLE__)
-/* Apple Silicon uses 16KB pages and 128 byte cache lines. */
+#if (defined(_M_ARM64) || defined(__aarch64__)) && (defined(__APPLE__) || defined(PCSX2_HOST_PAGE_16K))
+/* Apple Silicon uses 16KB pages and 128 byte cache lines. So does a Linux
+   aarch64 kernel built for 16KB pages (Raspberry Pi 5): build with
+   PCSX2_HOST_PAGE_16K there. The page size has to match the kernel's. */
 #define __pagesize 0x4000
 #define __pageshift 14
 #define __cachelinesize 128
